{"ts": 1789783505.478035, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "This is very revealing. Adding *almost any* third feature to {7,11} pushes the Sharpe from 0.738 into the 0.65\u20130.83 range. Features 26, 23, 35, 12, 14, 4 all had near-zero standalone Sharpes (0.108, 0.124, 0.124, 0.154, 0.105, 0.082) yet each *lifts* the pair.\n\nThat pattern is the signature of **variance reduction, not added signal**. Averaging in a third roughly-uncorrelated feature shrinks the denominator of the Sharpe ratio. The lift is mostly mechanical. The reason {7,11,16} tops the list at 0.831 is that f16 happened to have the largest standalone Sharpe among third features \u2014 plus whatever noise favored it in this particular sample.\n\nSo the honest decomposition of 0.831: a real component from f7 and f11, a mechanical averaging component available from any third feature, and a selection component from my having picked the best of many tries.\n\nLet me spend remaining effort confirming that f7 and f11 are the durable core, rather than chasing the last decimal."}
