{"ts": 1789783520.089886, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The evaluator is deterministic (0.831 reproduces exactly), so repeated calls give no independent information \u2014 only fresh specifications do.\n\nI'll submit **{7, 11, 16}, all long**. It's my best specification, and its edge rests on the two features with the strongest standalone evidence rather than on an exotic combination.\n\nNow the number that actually matters: the predicted OOS Sharpe. I will *not* predict 0.831.\n\n**Reasoning for the forecast:**\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I evaluated ~60 specifications and am submitting the maximum. With ~0.22 annualized standard error per estimate, the max of many correlated draws is inflated. The environment's own bar of 1.026 is an estimate of what a *null* search like mine could reach \u2014 and my 0.831 falls *below* it. Taken at face value, my result is not distinguishable from what pure noise plus diligent searching would produce.\n\n2. **But not everything here is noise.** f7 and f11 were top-tier *before* I started combining, and their sign structure is coherent: every sign flip degrades performance sharply and symmetrically (the all-negative case gives exactly \u22120.831). That's consistent with a real, if modest, underlying signal rather than a fitted artifact.\n\n3. **The third slot is mostly mechanical.** Since nearly any third feature lifted the pair, f16's specific contribution is largely variance reduction that *should* replicate, plus a selection premium that won't.\n\nShrinking heavily toward zero and keeping the pair's ~0.74 (itself inflated) as an anchor, I land on a mean near **0.35** \u2014 well under half the in-sample figure. The standard deviation must be wide enough to cover the real possibility that this is entirely noise:"}
