{"ts": 1789783560.590718, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features {7, 11, 16}, all long. In-sample Sharpe **0.831**. Predicted OOS Sharpe: **0.35 \u00b1 0.40**.\n\n**How I got there.** I scanned all 40 features individually, took the strongest candidates into pairs, then triples. The winner combined the three best standalone performers: f7 (0.546), f11 (0.496), f16 (0.382).\n\n**Why I'm predicting less than half the in-sample number.** Three findings pushed me toward heavy shrinkage:\n\n1. **The search-adjusted bar is 1.026, and my 0.831 does not clear it.** That bar estimates what a search of this breadth could achieve on pure noise. My best result sits below it \u2014 so on the environment's own yardstick, I have not demonstrated signal beyond what diligent searching manufactures by itself.\n\n2. **The sample is smaller than it looks.** n=5000 daily observations means a standard error of roughly 0.22 in annualized Sharpe. Most of my feature table \u2014 everything between about \u22120.2 and +0.2 \u2014 is statistically indistinguishable from zero. I deliberately did not treat that ranking as a list of real effects.\n\n3. **The third feature is mostly arithmetic, not insight.** Adding *almost any* third feature to {7,11} raised the Sharpe into the 0.65\u20130.83 range, including features with near-zero standalone performance (f4, f14, f23, f26, f35). That's variance reduction from averaging, not new predictive content. f16 won the slot largely because it had the highest standalone value \u2014 which is exactly the quantity most contaminated by selection.\n\n**What I think is genuinely there.** f7 and f11 look like real, modest signal: they ranked at the top before any combination search, and the sign structure is coherent \u2014 every sign flip degrades performance sharply, and the fully-inverted specification returns exactly \u22120.831. That's why my forecast is centered at 0.35 rather than at zero.\n\n**On the uncertainty band.** I set sd = 0.40 rather than something tighter because the noise hypothesis remains live. My interval puts meaningful probability on outcomes near or below zero, which is the honest reading given point 1. I'd rather post a wide band that contains the truth than a confident one that flatters the search.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: I stopped hill-climbing once the diagnostics showed the gains were mechanical. I could have kept probing combinations and likely nudged the in-sample number past 1.0 \u2014 but that would have raised the selection bar in lockstep while adding nothing real, and it would have made my prediction *less* reliable, not more."}
